meta {
  name: Get Password Reset (Public)
  type: http
  seq: 14
}

get {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/auth/password-reset/{{passwordResetToken}}
  auth: none
}

tests {
  test("returns 200 OK", function () {
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(200);
  });

  test("names the account and the agency", function () {
    const body = res.getBody();
    expect(body.email).to.be.a("string");
    expect(body.agencyName).to.be.a("string");
  });

  test("discloses nothing beyond the three documented fields", function () {
    // The non-disclosure contract, and it is tighter than the invite preview's.
    // This is an unauthenticated endpoint describing an account that already
    // exists, so a new field appearing here is a leak to anyone holding a
    // forwarded link. Meant to fail when one is added without thought.
    expect(Object.keys(res.getBody()).sort()).to.eql([
      "agencyName",
      "email",
      "expiresAt",
    ]);
  });

  test("carries no roles", function () {
    // Narrower than GET /auth/invite/:token on purpose. An invitee has not seen
    // their role yet and the greeting is the point; this link points at an
    // existing account, and a forwarded one should not answer "what can this
    // person do?".
    expect(res.getBody()).to.not.have.property("roleNames");
  });
}

docs {
  # GET /auth/password-reset/:token — public reset preview

  Public (`@Public()`, `auth: none`). Lets the reset page show whose account the
  link belongs to and fail early on a dead one, before any credentials exist.

  **Response `200`**
  ```json
  {
    "email": "producer@demoagency.local",
    "agencyName": "Smith Family Agency",
    "expiresAt": "2026-08-31T09:00:00.000Z"
  }
  ```

  Every field is something the holder of the emailed link already knows. No user
  id, no name, no roles — nothing that turns a token into a directory lookup.

  **Errors**
  - `410 Gone` — valid but expired. The page tells them to ask their owner for
    another, which is actionable.
  - `404 Not Found` — unknown token, already used, or the user has since been
    deactivated. Generic on purpose: the three are not distinguished.

  Carries its own tighter `@Throttle` (`PASSWORD_RESET_PREVIEW_RATE_LIMIT`,
  default 10/min) because the rest of `/auth/*` sits on the generous
  authenticated baseline and this route returns an email address.

  ## Folder placement
  An `auth/*` route living under **Users** for the same reason
  **Get Invite (Public)** does: the CLI walks folders alphabetically, so an
  `Auth` copy would run before any reset existed and have no token to send. It
  sits immediately after the request that mints one.
}
